<p>I enjoyed <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/23/opinion/ai-computer-chips-semiconductor-arizona-industry.html?unlocked_article_code=1.LVE.T4pO.KW_xY6NbyH_s&#038;smid=nytcore-ios-share">Friday&#8217;s New York Times piece by Dan Wang</a> about all the impacts, from the geopolitical to the personal, of Apple&#8217;s chipmaker Taiwan Semiconductor building fabs in Arizona as an attempt to boost American manufacturing capability and reduce risk of a future Chinese attack on Taiwan:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  In Arizona, I saw how Taiwanese engineers are training American workers to build some of the most complex technologies in the world. TSMC is doing for America what Apple did for China.</p>
<p>  And guess what? TSMC engineers and their families are having a great time. In interviews, they marveled at their new lives in the Sun Belt — the large homes, the friendly community, the opportunities for their kids.</p>
<p>  The question is how long this can last.
</p></blockquote>
<p>I didn&#8217;t realize that a majority of Arizona residents weren&#8217;t born here, but I&#8217;m writing this from the Phoenix-area home of a California transplant with people from Minnesota and Wisconsin in houses on either side. And yesterday I was south of Tucson with family members who also came from California. It&#8217;s that kind of place.</p>
